{"stem": "Read this paragraph.

(1) Last Saturday, our robotics team competed in a regional tournament at the Javits Center. (2) By the time we set up our robot, the first round already started. (3) We had to rush through our safety inspection, so we missed the practice run. (4) Despite the rough start, our robot scored more points than any other robot in the afternoon matches.

Which revision corrects the error in sentence 2?", "choices": [{"id": "ELA-REB-111-a", "label": "A", "text": "By the time we set up our robot, the first round has already started.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": ""Has started" is present perfect, which connects to the present. The tournament happened last Saturday, so this creates a tense shift."}, {"id": "ELA-REB-111-b", "label": "B", "text": "By the time we set up our robot, the first round had already started.", "isCorrect": true, "explanation": "Correct. The past perfect "had started" shows that the round began before the team finished setting up, which was itself in the past."}, {"id": "ELA-REB-111-c", "label": "C", "text": "By the time we set up our robot, the first round already starts.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": ""Starts" is present tense, which does not fit a story about last Saturday."}, {"id": "ELA-REB-111-d", "label": "D", "text": "By the time we had set up our robot, the first round already started.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": "This puts the past perfect on the wrong action. It suggests setting up came first, but the sentence means the round started first."}], "solutionExplanation": "When two past actions are described and one happened before the other, the earlier action uses the past perfect ("had" plus a past participle). Here the round started before the team finished setting up, so the round needs "had already started." Choice B is correct.", "commonTrap": "Choice D is tempting because it adds "had," which students know is the fix for this kind of sentence. But "had" must go with the earlier action. Ask which happened first: the round started first, so "had" belongs with "started."", "tags": ["RE-TEN"]}

{"stem": "Read this paragraph.

(1) Mae Jemison grew up in Chicago and studied chemical engineering at Stanford University. (2) She later earned a medical degree and served as a Peace Corps doctor in West Africa. (3) Jemison had dreamed of traveling to space since childhood, therefore, she applied to NASA's astronaut program. (4) In 1992, she became the first Black woman to travel into space.

Which edit is needed to correct the punctuation in the paragraph?", "choices": [{"id": "ELA-REB-131-a", "label": "A", "text": "Change the comma after "childhood" in sentence 3 to a semicolon.", "isCorrect": true, "explanation": "Correct. "Therefore" begins a second complete sentence. A semicolon before "therefore" joins the two sentences correctly, and the comma after "therefore" stays."}, {"id": "ELA-REB-131-b", "label": "B", "text": "Change the comma after "therefore" in sentence 3 to a semicolon.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": "This would put "therefore" with the first sentence and leave a comma splice between "childhood" and "therefore.""}, {"id": "ELA-REB-131-c", "label": "C", "text": "Insert a semicolon after "Chicago" in sentence 1.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": "The words after "Chicago" have no subject of their own, so they cannot stand alone. A semicolon there would be wrong."}, {"id": "ELA-REB-131-d", "label": "D", "text": "Change the comma after "1992" in sentence 4 to a semicolon.", "isCorrect": false, "explanation": ""In 1992" is an introductory phrase, not a complete sentence, so a comma is correct there."}], "solutionExplanation": "Sentence 3 contains two complete sentences: "Jemison had dreamed of traveling to space since childhood" and "therefore, she applied to NASA's astronaut program." "Therefore" is a transition word, not a conjunction, so it cannot join the sentences with a comma. Placing a semicolon before "therefore" fixes the comma splice.", "commonTrap": "Choice B is the trap. Students know a semicolon belongs near "therefore" but put it on the wrong side. The semicolon goes where the first sentence ends, before the transition word.", "tags": ["RE-SEM", "RE-RUN"]}

{"stem": "Read these sentences.

(1) Malika practiced piano every day after school.  
(2) She performed flawlessly at the winter recital.  What is the best way to combine the sentences to clarify the relationship between the ideas?", "choices": [{"id": "ELA-REB-005-a", "label": "A", "text": "Malika practiced piano every day after school, but she performed flawlessly at the winter recital.", "isCorrect": false}, {"id": "ELA-REB-005-b", "label": "B", "text": "Malika practiced piano every day after school, so she performed flawlessly at the winter recital.", "isCorrect": true}, {"id": "ELA-REB-005-c", "label": "C", "text": "Malika practiced piano every day after school, and she performed flawlessly at the winter recital.", "isCorrect": false}, {"id": "ELA-REB-005-d", "label": "D", "text": "Although Malika practiced piano every day after school, she performed flawlessly at the winter recital.", "isCorrect": false}], "solutionExplanation": "Choice A does not correctly clarify the relationship. The conjunction "but" suggests contrast or opposition between the two ideas, which is inaccurate, Malika's flawless performance was not surprising or contrary to her practice.  Choice B correctly clarifies the relationship. The conjunction "so" expresses cause and effect, Malika practiced every day, and as a result she performed flawlessly. This is the most logical relationship between the two ideas.  Choice C does not correctly clarify the relationship. The conjunction "and" simply links the two ideas without expressing any logical connection between them. It does not convey that the practice caused the strong performance.  Choice D does not correctly clarify the relationship. The conjunction "although" suggests that Malika performed flawlessly in spite of her practice, which is illogical.", "commonTrap": "Choice C is tempting because "and" is a neutral connector that makes the sentence grammatically correct. But the question asks for the combination that best clarifies the relationship between the ideas, and "and" does not convey cause and effect. "So" is the only conjunction that captures why the performance went well.", "tags": ["RE-SEN"]}
